  "plain": "In 1947, near the town of Roswell, New Mexico, something came down in the desert, and the story is often told as a crashed flying saucer that the military later called a weather balloon. A researcher named Bryce points out that the people at Roswell were running the only nuclear bomber base in the world at the time, so it seems odd to him that they could mistake a saucer for a balloon. A podcast host, Jesse Michels, makes a similar point in his own words: Roswell was not just a random desert, he says, but the largest concentration of nuclear weapons in the world at that moment. The two tell it a little differently, one stressing the bomber base and the other the weapons, but both use it to suggest that the people on the ground knew what they were looking at, and that a crash there would have mattered a great deal."
